user32: implement SkipPointerFrameMessages

Qt 6.5's Windows platform plugin statically imports user32.dll!SkipPointerFrameMessages and calls it
from QtPointerHandler::translateTouchEvent() (src/plugins/platforms/windows/qwindowspointerhandler.cpp)
after it has consumed a frame of WM_POINTER messages, so that no repeated messages are delivered for a
frame with several pointer ids.  Wine exported it only as a commented-out stub (user32.spec), so the
loader bound the import to an abort thunk and a touch/pen pointer frame killed the process with
"unimplemented function user32.dll.SkipPointerFrameMessages, aborting".

Windows' SkipPointerFrameMessages(pointerId) drops the remaining messages of that pointer's current
frame from the queue and returns TRUE on success.  Wine delivers pointer messages synchronously and
keeps no per-frame queue - GetPointerFrameInfo() and friends are served from a single stored
POINTER_INFO in win32u/input.c - so there is never anything left to skip: the call is a no-op that
reports success, which is what the caller expects.  dlls/user32/tests/input.c checks that the export
now exists and that calling it returns TRUE instead of aborting.
--- a/dlls/user32/user32.spec
+++ b/dlls/user32/user32.spec
@@ -1139,7 +1139,7 @@
 @ stdcall ShutdownBlockReasonDestroy(long)
 # @ stub ShutdownBlockReasonQuery
 # @ stub SignalRedirectionStartComplete
-# @ stub SkipPointerFrameMessages
+@ stdcall SkipPointerFrameMessages(long)
 # @ stub SoftModalMessageBox
 # @ stub SoundSentry
 @ stdcall SubtractRect(ptr ptr ptr)
--- a/dlls/user32/input.c
+++ b/dlls/user32/input.c
@@ -825,6 +825,22 @@
     return NtUserGetPointerInfoList( id, PT_PEN, 0, 0, sizeof(*info), entries, pointers, info );
 }
 
+/*****************************************************************************
+ * SkipPointerFrameMessages (USER32.@)
+ *
+ * Called by pointer stacks (Qt calls it from its touch frame loop) after a
+ * frame of WM_POINTER messages has been consumed, to drop the rest of the
+ * messages belonging to that frame from the queue.  Wine delivers pointer
+ * messages synchronously and keeps no per-frame queue, so there is never
+ * anything left to skip; report success rather than the failure a caller
+ * would otherwise have to deal with.
+ */
+BOOL WINAPI SkipPointerFrameMessages( UINT32 id )
+{
+    TRACE( "id %u\n", id );
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
 
